diagnosis_text:
There is no network fault between PC15 and PC16. All diagnostics confirm the two machines are communicating correctly. PC15 (172.18.51.15/24) and PC16 (172.18.51.16/24) both have their eth0 interfaces UP and carrier-active. Switch10's bridge is fully operational: eth1 (PC15's port, cd_sw10_fa1_1) and eth2 (PC16's port, cd_sw10_fa2_1) are both in STP **forwarding** state, and the bridge FDB has correctly learned both MACs. Bidirectional pings — PC15→PC16 and PC16→PC15 — complete with 4/4 packets received and sub-millisecond RTT (< 1.1 ms). ARP resolution is working on both ends, and neither host has any iptables rules that could block traffic.

The user's assertion that "PC16's port has gone dead" and that "nothing goes through AT ALL" is contradicted by every data-plane measurement. This is a false-premise ticket: the network segment is healthy and no corrective action is required. The root cause of the complaint is most likely a transient glitch (brief cable wiggle, brief switch port flap, or a Windows file-sharing permission issue unrelated to layer 2/3 connectivity) that had already self-resolved before the ticket was investigated, or a misidentification of the actual problem (e.g., a file-sharing/firewall issue on one of the PCs rather than a network outage). No switch port reconfiguration or cable change is needed.
=== END ===